+3. Stress test solution.py against the brute force: run hundreds of random cases. Run each program with a per-run timeout, e.g. `timeout 10 python3 solution.py < in.txt`, and compare outputs exactly (whitespace-trimmed per line is fine). Print the first mismatching input and both outputs when debugging.
+4. Time solution.py on maximum-size and worst-case inputs. It must comfortably finish within about 2 seconds. If it is too slow, switch to a better algorithm.
+5. Check the I/O contract: for stdin programs, full input parsing and exact output format; for `Solution` class tasks, the exact class/method signature and the needed imports (e.g. `from typing import *`). Verify the samples from the statement produce the expected outputs exactly.
+6. Fix any failure in solution.py (using the editor tool) and rerun the checks. Never leave debug prints or scratch code in solution.py.
+
+When finished, end your final message with exactly one of these lines:
+VERDICT: PASS   (no changes were needed)
+VERDICT: FIXED  (you made changes to solution.py)
